# ---------------------------------------------------------------- successor_hand

== the first week under the new head
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-90
calls: WR
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: colleague, regular, friend
cast_want: successor_hand
earth: the one you chose takes over your place, and in the first week makes clear that much will change
times: Those who hand on what they built often find the hardest part is letting the next one make it their own (Erikson 1950)
gap: 3-8
scene: The day has come: {successor} takes over at {place}, with {Ns} blessing. In the first week {successor} makes it clear that much will change, starting with things {N} built with {Ns} own hands. People keep glancing at {N} to see how {N} will take it.
scene W: A handover means something only if it is a real one; {N} gave {Ns} word, and the word was all of it.
scene U: {N} knows things about {place} that {successor} has not learned yet, and they could be passed on, or kept back.
scene B: {N} built a great deal of standing here, and it does not vanish because the keys changed hands.
scene R: Watching someone young tear into the work with fresh hands, {N} feels proud and sore at once.
scene G: {place} was handed to {N} once by someone who must have felt just this; the turn has come round.
- W | hand over every key and every duty on the agreed day, and step back fully | hand: chosen | lever: exit | v: conformity, universalism | foot: door kept your word | chance: 80
- U | leave {successor} a full account of how things run and why, then let go | hand: chosen | lever: loyalty | v: achievement, benevolence | foot: means time+.06 | chance: 75
- B | stay on the board, close enough to steer the biggest decisions | hand: stay | lever: office | v: power, security | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 70
- R | tell {successor} to change whatever needs changing, and mean every word | hand: chosen | lever: loyalty | v: self-direction, benevolence | foot: person friend | chance: 75
- G | keep coming to {place} as an ordinary regular, in the old seat | hand: stay | lever: loyalty | v: tradition, security | foot: lesson glad to be a regular again | chance: 75
- W>B | agree with {successor} in writing what is {successor}'s to change and what needs the members' vote | hand: chosen | lever: voice | v: conformity, power | foot: person colleague | chance: 60
- U>R | offer to advise only when asked, and keep the opinions for a notebook | hand: chosen | lever: loyalty | v: self-direction, benevolence | foot: means ties-.03 | chance: 65
- B>G | keep a hand in the old networks, so {successor} still needs {N} for the town's doors | hand: stay | lever: subvert | v: power, security | foot: person go-between | chance: 70
- R>W | throw a farewell night, and ask everyone to back {successor} as they backed {N} | hand: chosen | lever: loyalty | v: hedonism, universalism | foot: place gather.great | chance: 65
- G>U | stay one more season beside {successor}, so the old ways are learned before they are changed | hand: stay | lever: loyalty | v: tradition, achievement | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 65
works: {successor} changes a great deal, keeps a little, and {place} goes on with a life of its own.
works: Now and then {successor} asks {N} for a word, listens, and does something else, and {N} finds that is enough.
fails: Watching {Ns} work undone stings more than {N} expected, and {N} stays away from {place} for a while.
fails: People at {place} take sides between the old way and the new, and {successor} blames {N} for it.

== a year after the keys changed hands
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-90
calls: UG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community, friends
horizon: years
roles: colleague, regular, elder
cast_want: successor_hand
earth: a year after you handed on your place, it is hard to recognise, and the old regulars ask you to step in
times: A successor carries on in their own direction, and the further it is from the founder's, the less of the founder's mark the place keeps (Erikson 1950; Hirschman 1970)
gap: 3-8
scene: A year after {N} handed {place} to {successor}, it is hard to recognise. {successor} has gone a very different way, and a group of old regulars has come to {N} asking {N} to say something, or even to come back. {successor} has not asked {N} for anything.
scene W: The post was handed on properly, and a handover that can be taken back was never a handover.
scene U: Different is not the same as worse; {N} would like to know which this is before saying a word.
scene B: The old regulars are offering {N} a lever, and {N} has not forgotten how to use one.
scene R: It hurts to see {place} like this, and {N} is not built to keep quiet when something hurts.
scene G: The old regulars still need somewhere that feels like theirs, whatever becomes of {place}.
